    {
      "id": "JbQd-IenSwI89ZDRoOG0P",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1760.1883727379522,
      "y": 3914.487352945048,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 1200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b01",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1589923310,
      "version": 2504,
      "versionNonce": 1440466506,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1775943516915,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How can we ensure comments are broadcasted to viewers in real-time?\n\n\nInstead of client requesting new comments, we can use a push based model.\nThis way server can push new comments to client as soon as they are created.\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Websockets\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Client opens a connection to the server and keeps it open\n→ Server keeps connection open & sends new comments without additional requests\n→ When a new comment arrives, the Comment Management Server distributes it to all\nclients, enabling them to update the comment feed\n→ More efficient as it eliminates polling and enables the server to immediately push\nnew comments to the client upon creation\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Good solution, for real-time chat applications that have a more balanced read/write\nratio, they are optimal\n→ Here read/write ratio is not balanced. Comment creation is infrequent, most viewers\nwill not post comments, they will view/read all comments.\n→ doesn't make sense to open a two-way communication channel for each viewer,\ngiven that the overhead of maintaining the connection is high\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Server Sent Events (SSE)\n\nSSE is a persistent connection that operates over standard HTTP, making it simpler to\nimplement than WebSockets\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Server pushes data to the client in real-time\n→ Client-to-server communication happens over regular HTTP requests\n→ Infrequent comment creation can use POST requests\n→ Frequent reads benefit from SSE's efficient one-way streaming\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Some proxies & load balancers lack support for streaming responses, (leading to\nbuffering issues that are difficult to track down)\n→ Browsers have limits on concurrent SSE connections per domain, limiting number of\nlive videos watched simultaneously\n→ Long-lived nature of SSE connections complicates monitoring and debugging efforts.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How can we ensure comments are broadcasted to viewers in real-time?\n\n\nInstead of client requesting new comments, we can use a push based model.\nThis way server can push new comments to client as soon as they are created.\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Websockets\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Client opens a connection to the server and keeps it open\n→ Server keeps connection open & sends new comments without additional requests\n→ When a new comment arrives, the Comment Management Server distributes it to all clients, enabling them to update the comment feed\n→ More efficient as it eliminates polling and enables the server to immediately push new comments to the client upon creation\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Good solution, for real-time chat applications that have a more balanced read/write ratio, they are optimal\n→ Here read/write ratio is not balanced. Comment creation is infrequent, most viewers will not post comments, they will view/read all comments.\n→ doesn't make sense to open a two-way communication channel for each viewer, given that the overhead of maintaining the connection is high\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Server Sent Events (SSE)\n\nSSE is a persistent connection that operates over standard HTTP, making it simpler to implement than WebSockets\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Server pushes data to the client in real-time\n→ Client-to-server communication happens over regular HTTP requests\n→ Infrequent comment creation can use POST requests\n→ Frequent reads benefit from SSE's efficient one-way streaming\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Some proxies & load balancers lack support for streaming responses, (leading to buffering issues that are difficult to track down)\n→ Browsers have limits on concurrent SSE connections per domain, limiting number of live videos watched simultaneously\n→ Long-lived nature of SSE connections complicates monitoring and debugging efforts.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pIPigoQ79kMTSulNiV_td",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2800.212109999402,
      "y": 3919.7426906008513,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 3325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b06",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1412218762,
      "version": 5635,
      "versionNonce": 1947204810,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1775948871505,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2) How will the system scale to support millions of concurrent viewers?\n\n\nPart 1: Server Coordination\n\nWhen viewers of the same video are spread across multiple servers, how do those\nservers share comments with each other?\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Naive Pub/Sub: Every Server Processes Every Comment\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Separate read and write traffic because write traffic is much lower than the read\ntraffic and we need to scale them independently\n→ When client connects to Realtime Messaging Server, sends the videoID. The server\nmaintains a mapping in local memory:\n\n{\n    \"liveVideoId1\": [\"sseConnection1\", \"sseConnection2\"],\n    \"liveVideoId2\": [\"sseConnection3\", \"sseConnection4\"]\n}\n\n→ To signal clients about new messages we use pub/sub (single channel)\n        → Comment service publishes a message to a channel whenever a new comment is\ncreated { videoId:, comment: }\n        →  Realtime Servers subscribe to channel of ALL videos & sends the comment to\nall viewers watching that video\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Works, but it's inefficient at scale.\n→ Realtime Server processes every comment across all live videos\n→ Leads to wasted compute & impractical at Facebook scale\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Partitioned Pub/Sub with Viewer Co-location\n\nImprove efficiency by partitioning comment stream into different channels by videoId.\nEach Realtime Server subscribes only to required channels, determined by viewers\nconnected to it\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Having 1 channel / video, consumes lot of resources, use a hashing function to\ndistribute comments across N channels:\n\nhash(liveVideoId) % N.\n\nThis ensures a reasonable upper bound on channel count.\n\n→ With round-robin LB, servers could end up with viewers subscribed to all streams,\nforcing it to subscribe to all channels, replicating inefficiency we tried to avoid\n→ Solution is intelligent routing\n        → Layer 7 LB can use consistent hashing based on liveVideoId to route viewers of a\nvideo to the same server\n        → Alternatively, can use Zookeeper to store mapping of liveVideoId to server,\ngiving more flexibility at the cost of operational complexity.\n\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Managing coordination between LB & pub/sub subscriptions:\n        → Server's viewer composition changes, needs to update subscriptions accordingly\n        → When servers scale up or down, routing mappings need to stay in sync\n\n\n\nPart 2: Mega-Streams\n\nMega-stream has 5,000 comments per second and the average comment is 4 words\nIf we show the last 20 messages on screen, each message appears for about 4\nmilliseconds before being pushed off. No human can actually read that fast.\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Show a Representative Subset\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Users don't need to see every comment. They just need to see enough to feel the\nenergy of the crowd\n→ Sample the comment stream and only deliver a subset to each viewer,\n        → Use a sampling rate that adjusts based on comment velocity\n        → Each viewer receives roughly the same number of comments / second regardless\nof how fast they're actually being posted, creating a consistent & readable experience\n        → Use a sampling rate that adjusts based on comment velocity\n→ Can use a sampling strategy too, rather than just random, we can prioritize\n        → Comments from users the viewer follows\n        → Comments getting reactions\n        → Comments from verified accounts\n\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ While sampling reduces load significantly, still maintain SSE connections to millions\nof concurrent viewers and push updates in real-time\n→ Infrastructure costs, remain substantial\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: CDN-Based Delivery with Periodic Snapshots\n\nfor mega-streams, instead of pushing comments over SSE, we switch to pull-based\nmodel using CDNs\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ On server, we maintain ring buffer of most recent 100-200 comments for each mega-\nstream\n→ Every second (or more frequently), we snapshot the buffer & write to Redis or CDN\norigin (which distributes snapshot globally, caching at edge locations)\n→ On client, instead of SSE connection, app polls CDN every second for latest snapshot\n→ When a new snapshot arrives,\n        → Smoothly animates new comments, spacing them over polling interval to\nsimulate a continuous stream\n        → Client can even use timestamps to space comments according to actual timing,\nmaking experience feel organic\n→ We can set a threshold dynamically too, when a stream crosses 100,000 concurrent\nviewers or 500 comments / second, we automatically flip from SSE to CDN delivery.\nClient handles this transition seamlessly\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Tradeoff is latency. Comments delivered with up to 1-2 seconds of delay rather than\nsub-200ms. But this doesn't matter for mega-streams because users can't meaningfully\nengage with individual comments anyway.\n\n→ For \"read your own write\" consistency, handled by locally adding user's own\ncomments into their feed immediately, independent of CDN polled feed.\n→ Another subtle complexity is the transition back to SSE when viewership drops, add\nhysteresis to threshold logic to prevent flapping between modes as viewership hovers\naround the cutoff point",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2) How will the system scale to support millions of concurrent viewers?\n\n\nPart 1: Server Coordination\n\nWhen viewers of the same video are spread across multiple servers, how do those servers share comments with each other?\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Naive Pub/Sub: Every Server Processes Every Comment\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Separate read and write traffic because write traffic is much lower than the read traffic and we need to scale them independently\n→ When client connects to Realtime Messaging Server, sends the videoID. The server maintains a mapping in local memory:\n\n{\n    \"liveVideoId1\": [\"sseConnection1\", \"sseConnection2\"],\n    \"liveVideoId2\": [\"sseConnection3\", \"sseConnection4\"]\n}\n\n→ To signal clients about new messages we use pub/sub (single channel)\n        → Comment service publishes a message to a channel whenever a new comment is created { videoId:, comment: }\n        →  Realtime Servers subscribe to channel of ALL videos & sends the comment to all viewers watching that video\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Works, but it's inefficient at scale.\n→ Realtime Server processes every comment across all live videos\n→ Leads to wasted compute & impractical at Facebook scale\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Partitioned Pub/Sub with Viewer Co-location\n\nImprove efficiency by partitioning comment stream into different channels by videoId. Each Realtime Server subscribes only to required channels, determined by viewers connected to it\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Having 1 channel / video, consumes lot of resources, use a hashing function to distribute comments across N channels:\n\nhash(liveVideoId) % N.\n\nThis ensures a reasonable upper bound on channel count.\n\n→ With round-robin LB, servers could end up with viewers subscribed to all streams, forcing it to subscribe to all channels, replicating inefficiency we tried to avoid\n→ Solution is intelligent routing\n        → Layer 7 LB can use consistent hashing based on liveVideoId to route viewers of a video to the same server\n        → Alternatively, can use Zookeeper to store mapping of liveVideoId to server, giving more flexibility at the cost of operational complexity.\n\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Managing coordination between LB & pub/sub subscriptions:\n        → Server's viewer composition changes, needs to update subscriptions accordingly\n        → When servers scale up or down, routing mappings need to stay in sync\n\n\n\nPart 2: Mega-Streams\n\nMega-stream has 5,000 comments per second and the average comment is 4 words\nIf we show the last 20 messages on screen, each message appears for about 4 milliseconds before being pushed off. No human can actually read that fast.\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Show a Representative Subset\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Users don't need to see every comment. They just need to see enough to feel the energy of the crowd\n→ Sample the comment stream and only deliver a subset to each viewer,\n        → Use a sampling rate that adjusts based on comment velocity\n        → Each viewer receives roughly the same number of comments / second regardless of how fast they're actually being posted, creating a consistent & readable experience\n        → Use a sampling rate that adjusts based on comment velocity\n→ Can use a sampling strategy too, rather than just random, we can prioritize\n        → Comments from users the viewer follows\n        → Comments getting reactions\n        → Comments from verified accounts\n\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ While sampling reduces load significantly, still maintain SSE connections to millions of concurrent viewers and push updates in real-time\n→ Infrastructure costs, remain substantial\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: CDN-Based Delivery with Periodic Snapshots\n\nfor mega-streams, instead of pushing comments over SSE, we switch to pull-based model using CDNs\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ On server, we maintain ring buffer of most recent 100-200 comments for each mega-stream\n→ Every second (or more frequently), we snapshot the buffer & write to Redis or CDN origin (which distributes snapshot globally, caching at edge locations)\n→ On client, instead of SSE connection, app polls CDN every second for latest snapshot\n→ When a new snapshot arrives,\n        → Smoothly animates new comments, spacing them over polling interval to simulate a continuous stream\n        → Client can even use timestamps to space comments according to actual timing, making experience feel organic\n→ We can set a threshold dynamically too, when a stream crosses 100,000 concurrent viewers or 500 comments / second, we automatically flip from SSE to CDN delivery. Client handles this transition seamlessly\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Tradeoff is latency. Comments delivered with up to 1-2 seconds of delay rather than sub-200ms. But this doesn't matter for mega-streams because users can't meaningfully engage with individual comments anyway.\n\n→ For \"read your own write\" consistency, handled by locally adding user's own comments into their feed immediately, independent of CDN polled feed.\n→ Another subtle complexity is the transition back to SSE when viewership drops, add hysteresis to threshold logic to prevent flapping between modes as viewership hovers around the cutoff point",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3YCUGV7y8MJ4GcRq52F-n",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3768.0656623528703,
      "y": 3907.0794759831215,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 1300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b07",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1000709334,
      "version": 4140,
      "versionNonce": 192808394,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1775948867956,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3) How do we handle client disconnections and ensure viewers don't miss comments?\n\n\nSystem needs to handle network interruptions gracefully, ensuring users can catch up\non what they missed without losing their place in the stream.\n\n\n🟡 Bad Solution: Ignore Disconnections and Hope for the Best\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Do nothing special, on reconnect, simply start receiving new comments from that\nmoment forward\n→ Comments posted during their disconnection are lost to them\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Poor UX, especially for brief disconnections\n→ For longer disconnections, users might return to a feed that's completely different\nfrom where they left off, with no context about what happened in between\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Last-Event-ID with Client-Side Tracking\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ For more control, the client can track the last comment ID locally (in localStorage for\nweb, or app storage for mobile) and explicitly request catch-up via HTTP: GET\n/comments/:liveVideoId?since={last_comment_id}&limit=100, allowing for better UX on\nreconnection:\n        → Client can animate them smoothly at 2-3x speed, rather than dumping instantly\n        → Show \"You missed 47 comments\" with an option to jump to live\n\n→ Mobile, when app is going to be backgrounded, can preemptively disconnect the SSE\nand record its position, When foregrounded, it reconnects and catches up\n        → More efficient than maintaining a connection while backgrounded, which most\nmobile OS's throttle anyway.\n\n→ Set practical limits on how far back we'll replay. Replaying the last 5 minutes is\nreasonable, with graceful degradation for longer gaps.\n\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ If reconnect happens to different Realtime Messaging Server(due to load balancing),\nserver needs to access comment history. (Shared Redis cache gives any server ability to\nreplay recent comments for any video.)\n\n→ Complexity of coordinating between the SSE stream and HTTP catch-up to avoid\nduplicates or gaps. (Client needs to deduplicate based on comment IDs and merge\ncorrectly.)\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3) How do we handle client disconnections and ensure viewers don't miss comments?\n\n\nSystem needs to handle network interruptions gracefully, ensuring users can catch up on what they missed without losing their place in the stream.\n\n\n🟡 Bad Solution: Ignore Disconnections and Hope for the Best\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ Do nothing special, on reconnect, simply start receiving new comments from that moment forward\n→ Comments posted during their disconnection are lost to them\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ Poor UX, especially for brief disconnections\n→ For longer disconnections, users might return to a feed that's completely different from where they left off, with no context about what happened in between\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Last-Event-ID with Client-Side Tracking\n\n🔧 Approach\n\n→ For more control, the client can track the last comment ID locally (in localStorage for web, or app storage for mobile) and explicitly request catch-up via HTTP: GET /comments/:liveVideoId?since={last_comment_id}&limit=100, allowing for better UX on reconnection:\n        → Client can animate them smoothly at 2-3x speed, rather than dumping instantly\n        → Show \"You missed 47 comments\" with an option to jump to live\n\n→ Mobile, when app is going to be backgrounded, can preemptively disconnect the SSE and record its position, When foregrounded, it reconnects and catches up\n        → More efficient than maintaining a connection while backgrounded, which most mobile OS's throttle anyway.\n\n→ Set practical limits on how far back we'll replay. Replaying the last 5 minutes is reasonable, with graceful degradation for longer gaps.\n\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\n→ If reconnect happens to different Realtime Messaging Server(due to load balancing), server needs to access comment history. (Shared Redis cache gives any server ability to replay recent comments for any video.)\n\n→ Complexity of coordinating between the SSE stream and HTTP catch-up to avoid duplicates or gaps. (Client needs to deduplicate based on comment IDs and merge correctly.)\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
