    {
      "id": "HZ03GW7T9npNn8HkEcVyg",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3812.7839714355327,
      "y": 595.390102495859,
      "width": 801.67919921875,
      "height": 1075,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2s",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 963898774,
      "version": 2787,
      "versionNonce": 1133229066,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1775951353761,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "DynamoDB Shard Key Calculation\n\n\nRandom Sharding\n\nshard = random(0, N)  →  \"video123#3\"\n→ Writes spread evenly across N shards\n→ Simple to implement\n→ Problem: reads must query all N shards — no way to know where a comment landed\n\n\nDeterministic Sharding ✅\n\nshard = videoId + \"#\" + (hash(commentId) % N)  →  \"video123#2\"\n→ Same even write distribution\n→ Given any commentId, you can compute exactly which shard it's on\n→ All possible shard keys for a video are predictable: video123#0 through video123#(N-\n1)\n\n\nScatter-Gather on Read\n\nquery GSI where PK = \"video123#0\" AND createdAt < cursor\nquery GSI where PK = \"video123#1\" AND createdAt < cursor\n...\nquery GSI where PK = \"video123#(N-1)\" AND createdAt < cursor\n\n→ Fire all N queries in parallel → merge-sort by createdAt → take top 10\n→ Scatter is deterministic, not a full table scan — that's what makes it viable\n\n\nTuning N 🎛️\n\n→ DynamoDB partition limit ~1000 writes/sec\n→ Viral video peaking at 5000 comments/sec → minimum N=5, realistically N=10-20\nwith headroom\n→ At mega-stream scale, sidestep the problem entirely with CDN snapshot model —\nstop writing comments individually, snapshot the ring buffer periodically instead\n\n\nTradeoff:\nMore shards → writes spread thinner, handles higher velocity — but read latency and\ncomplexity grow linearly with N ⚖️",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "4nZjJ86ACOS76NNCd_FYI",
      "originalText": "DynamoDB Shard Key Calculation\n\n\nRandom Sharding\n\nshard = random(0, N)  →  \"video123#3\"\n→ Writes spread evenly across N shards\n→ Simple to implement\n→ Problem: reads must query all N shards — no way to know where a comment landed\n\n\nDeterministic Sharding ✅\n\nshard = videoId + \"#\" + (hash(commentId) % N)  →  \"video123#2\"\n→ Same even write distribution\n→ Given any commentId, you can compute exactly which shard it's on\n→ All possible shard keys for a video are predictable: video123#0 through video123#(N-1)\n\n\nScatter-Gather on Read\n\nquery GSI where PK = \"video123#0\" AND createdAt < cursor\nquery GSI where PK = \"video123#1\" AND createdAt < cursor\n...\nquery GSI where PK = \"video123#(N-1)\" AND createdAt < cursor\n\n→ Fire all N queries in parallel → merge-sort by createdAt → take top 10\n→ Scatter is deterministic, not a full table scan — that's what makes it viable\n\n\nTuning N 🎛️\n\n→ DynamoDB partition limit ~1000 writes/sec\n→ Viral video peaking at 5000 comments/sec → minimum N=5, realistically N=10-20 with headroom\n→ At mega-stream scale, sidestep the problem entirely with CDN snapshot model — stop writing comments individually, snapshot the ring buffer periodically instead\n\n\nTradeoff:\nMore shards → writes spread thinner, handles higher velocity — but read latency and complexity grow linearly with N ⚖️",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "GY5yar5FSVK2N4zW9yaiR",
