    {
      "id": "a2CRlgSlheypudDz1ysVX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5131.22189854629,
      "y": 882.9642213569743,
      "width": 801.3592529296875,
      "height": 775,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2u",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 822166410,
      "version": 3264,
      "versionNonce": 694372758,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1775950155528,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Offset Pagination ❌\n\n→ Inefficient at scale — DB must count all preceding rows for every query\n→ Unstable under mutations:\n        → Deleted comment → missed comments in results\n        → Added comment → duplicate comments appear\n→ Gets worse as comment volume grows 📈\n\n\nCursor Pagination ✅\n\nCursor = unique identifier pointing to a specific item in results\n\n→ No row-counting needed — far more efficient (cursor column should be indexed)\n→ Performance remains consistent as comment volume grow\n→ Stable — additions/deletions don't shift results; cursor still points to correct item\n\n\nOrdering Detail:\n\n→ ORDER BY created_at DESC (newest first) is the common case\n→ New comments sort to position 0, pushing everything down — so they'd never appear\nnaturally via pagination\n→ This is fine ✅ — new comments are handled by SSE, not pagination; pagination is\nonly for scrolling through historical comments\n\n\nTradeoff:\nCursor pagination is harder to implement and doesn't support jumping to arbitrary pages\n(e.g. \"go to page 5\") — but for infinite scroll / comment history, this is rarely needed and\nstability + performance wins ⚖️",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "GY5yar5FSVK2N4zW9yaiR",
      "originalText": "Offset Pagination ❌\n\n→ Inefficient at scale — DB must count all preceding rows for every query\n→ Unstable under mutations:\n        → Deleted comment → missed comments in results\n        → Added comment → duplicate comments appear\n→ Gets worse as comment volume grows 📈\n\n\nCursor Pagination ✅\n\nCursor = unique identifier pointing to a specific item in results\n\n→ No row-counting needed — far more efficient (cursor column should be indexed)\n→ Performance remains consistent as comment volume grow\n→ Stable — additions/deletions don't shift results; cursor still points to correct item\n\n\nOrdering Detail:\n\n→ ORDER BY created_at DESC (newest first) is the common case\n→ New comments sort to position 0, pushing everything down — so they'd never appear naturally via pagination\n→ This is fine ✅ — new comments are handled by SSE, not pagination; pagination is only for scrolling through historical comments\n\n\nTradeoff:\nCursor pagination is harder to implement and doesn't support jumping to arbitrary pages (e.g. \"go to page 5\") — but for infinite scroll / comment history, this is rarely needed and stability + performance wins ⚖️",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
