      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "BEFNcn7tJdlIdFg63eam6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1739.8915329617957,
      "y": 5247.9017013938455,
      "width": 815.519287109375,
      "height": 950,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3E",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1317382038,
      "version": 322,
      "versionNonce": 1438946390,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1775941808360,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "SSE Observability Challenges 🔍\n\n\nDuration Metrics Break Down\n\n→ Standard latency dashboards flag long SSE connections as \"slow\" — they're actually\nhealthy\n→ p95 latency metrics become misleading and alarming for the wrong reasons\n\nLog Volume is Deceptive 📋\n→ Only 2 log entries per connection: open and close — nothing in between\n→ 500 events over 30 mins = 1 log line; can't tell if connection was healthy or silent for 28\nof those minutes\n\nLoad Balancer Timeouts ⚠️\n→ nginx/ALBs default read timeout is ~60s — silently kills quiet SSE connections\n→ Both client and server believe connection is still alive (it isn't)\n→ Fix requires explicit keepalive events (: ping\\n\\n) + tuned timeout configs\n→ Diagnosing why clients disconnect requires tracing through multiple infrastructure\nlayers\n\nConnection Count = Active Resource Drain\n→ Each SSE client holds: a file descriptor + thread/async context + server memory — for\nthe entire session\n→ 10,000 users open = 10,000 persistent connections right now 📡\n→ Won't surface naturally in request-rate metrics — needs separate instrumentation\n\nDebugging Stuck Connections is Hard\n→ TCP still up + server writing successfully + EventSource still open = no obvious signal\nanything is wrong\n→ Requires application-level heartbeats and client-side silence detection — extra code to\nwrite and monitor\n\nTradeoff:\nHTTP request/response gives you observability for free — successes, failures, and latency\nall map cleanly to existing tooling. SSE trades that away for a persistent connection\nmodel, meaning you must explicitly instrument duration, liveness, and per-connection\nhealth yourself rather than inheriting it from your HTTP metrics stack ⚖️",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "IvkitoLCIVE2wzWi55sgR",
      "originalText": "SSE Observability Challenges 🔍\n\n\nDuration Metrics Break Down\n\n→ Standard latency dashboards flag long SSE connections as \"slow\" — they're actually healthy\n→ p95 latency metrics become misleading and alarming for the wrong reasons\n\nLog Volume is Deceptive 📋\n→ Only 2 log entries per connection: open and close — nothing in between\n→ 500 events over 30 mins = 1 log line; can't tell if connection was healthy or silent for 28 of those minutes\n\nLoad Balancer Timeouts ⚠️\n→ nginx/ALBs default read timeout is ~60s — silently kills quiet SSE connections\n→ Both client and server believe connection is still alive (it isn't)\n→ Fix requires explicit keepalive events (: ping\\n\\n) + tuned timeout configs\n→ Diagnosing why clients disconnect requires tracing through multiple infrastructure layers\n\nConnection Count = Active Resource Drain\n→ Each SSE client holds: a file descriptor + thread/async context + server memory — for the entire session\n→ 10,000 users open = 10,000 persistent connections right now 📡\n→ Won't surface naturally in request-rate metrics — needs separate instrumentation\n\nDebugging Stuck Connections is Hard\n→ TCP still up + server writing successfully + EventSource still open = no obvious signal anything is wrong\n→ Requires application-level heartbeats and client-side silence detection — extra code to write and monitor\n\nTradeoff:\nHTTP request/response gives you observability for free — successes, failures, and latency all map cleanly to existing tooling. SSE trades that away for a persistent connection model, meaning you must explicitly instrument duration, liveness, and per-connection health yourself rather than inheriting it from your HTTP metrics stack ⚖️",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Q8klulN5R9S42Zy3rCfLT",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 3783.744515579794,
      "y": 6445.576485004113,
      "width": 825.5660271246614,
      "height": 1025.8177818255722,
